    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Real-Time Progress Prediction in Reasoning <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Raaschou-Jensen%2C+H+P+L">Hans Peter Lyngsøe Raaschou-Jensen</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Fierro%2C+C">Constanza Fierro</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=S%C3%B8gaard%2C+A">Anders Søgaard</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2506.23274v4-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Recent reasoning <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span>, particularly those that employ long latent chains of thought, achieve strong performance on complex <span class="search-hit mathjax">agentic</span> tasks. However, as these models operate over increasingly long time horizons, their internal progress becomes opaque to users, making expe&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2506.23274v4-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2506.23274v4-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2506.23274v4-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Recent reasoning <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span>, particularly those that employ long latent chains of thought, achieve strong performance on complex <span class="search-hit mathjax">agentic</span> tasks. However, as these models operate over increasingly long time horizons, their internal progress becomes opaque to users, making expectation management and real-time oversight difficult. In this work, we investigate whether real-time progress prediction is feasible for such models. We first test whether hidden states encode progress information by discretizing reasoning trajectories and training a linear probe to classify reasoning states. We then fine-tune models to generate progress estimates from 0--100\% during chain-of-thought reasoning. Our strongest progress-reporting checkpoint reaches 0.161 MAE on mathematical reasoning traces and outperforms position baselines in this setting. Finally, we quantify the intrinsic ambiguity of progress labels by measuring how much the implied progress value varies from the same partial rollout. This ambiguity is lowest for Qwen3-4B, whose continuations produce the smallest rollout dispersion, suggesting that larger models can make progress labels more stable by reducing variation in remaining solution length.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2506.23274v4-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2506.23274v4-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 26 May, 2026; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 29 June, 2025;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> June 2025.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
